# Full Pyparsing original frontend comparison v15

This fixed run uses the complete original public Pyparsing inventory and the
exact authored comment-only `pyparsing/helpers.py` change retained in v13. Scope
is 286 regular files, 154 Python files and one opaque symlink. It does not change
the source mutation, graph predicate, source-read guards, proof scope or worker
deadline. The isolated source profile is v7 and its full source manifest is
copied from the completed original-observation qualification before dispatch.

Three worker roles each have a prospective 900-second process limit. The
baseline cache bootstrap and changed cold worker start concurrently on the
shared host. The incremental worker starts only after the baseline bootstrap
finishes successfully and its exact parent state, root, all-block storage and
original-observation sidecar identities are bound into `parent_binding.json`.

The baseline bootstrap performs a new native full scan of the original source
and produces original observations under v7. It then restores the exact original
v13 baseline state and every bundle block, verifies the full native bundle and
requires byte-identical newly scanned state before reusing the baseline bundle.
This avoids another construction of an already verified identical bundle. The
original v13 baseline preparation cost of 656.008 seconds remains charged and
its receipt is retained. The new cache creation, native restore and full
verification, subsequent full verification and persistence costs are included
in the bootstrap worker receipt. They are additional work, not free reuse.

The changed cold worker produces a fresh full bundle and original-observation
sidecar. The incremental worker restores and fully re-verifies the persisted
baseline state and all blocks, reads the exact bound original-observation bytes,
and supplies the actual parent state, parent bundle and sidecar to the native
scanner/builder. Every current file is still captured and verified. Scanner
diagnostics record original observation reuse, rescans, edges and sidecar bytes.

Both changed outputs must have identical serialized state and root, every
actual CID-to-block byte pair, full block count/byte count and original-observation
sidecar bytes. They must also pass native fresh, stale expected-state, stale
source, changed source, and between-read change controls. Both runtime/source
inventories are checked before and after the worker. Durable output hashes and
per-stage process wall/CPU times are retained, including observation encoding and
decoding within the named scan stage and file persistence within the named
persistence stage. One-time enrollment copying, source/runtime guards and
freeze preparation are separately timed. Overlapping process times are not
summed into an elapsed-time speedup.

Failed, timed-out and partial results remain explicit; no deadline extension or
unrecorded retry is permitted. The independent final audit reads all actual
block bytes and compares the public root/state to the retained v13/v14 cold
output. This is a source-bound operational comparison under honest producer
custody, not an execution theorem, provider token-saving result or general
incremental speedup claim.
